===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[tree]] (known as the [[calabash tree]]; ''[[Crescentia cujete]]'') [[native#Adjective|native]] to [[Central America|Central]] and [[South America]], the [[West Indies]], and southern [[Florida]], [[bear#Verb|bearing]] [[large]], [[round#Adjective|round]] [[fruit#Noun|fruit]] used to make [[container]]s ''(sense 3)''; the fruit of this tree.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Alexander de Humboldt [''i.e.'', {{w|Alexander von Humboldt}}]|author2=Aimé Bonpland|authorlink2=Aimé Bonpland|chapter=XXI|translator={{w|Helen Maria Williams}}|title=Personal Narrative of Travels to the Equinoctial Regions of the New Continent, during the Years 1799–1804. [...] Written in French{{nb...|by Alexander de Humboldt}}, and Translated into English{{nb...|by Helen Maria Williams.}}|location=London|publisher=Printed for [[w:Longman|Longman, Hurst, Rees, Orme, and Brown]],{{nb...|Paternoster Row.}}|year=1821|volume=V|section=book VII|page=129|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=vD0UAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA129|oclc=861227170|passage=As we were absolutely destitute of large vessels, to contain and mix liquids, we poured, by means of a ''tutuma'' (fruit of ''crescentia cujete'', '''calabash'''), the water of the river into one of the holes of the rock. To this we added sugar, and the juice of acid fruits. In a few minutes we had an excellent beverage, which was almost a refinement of luxury in that wild spot; [...]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|chapter=Goso, the Teacher|translator={{w|Edward Steere}}|title=Swahili Tales, as Told by Natives of Zanzibar. With an English Translation|location=London|publisher=[[w:George Bell & Sons|Bell & Daldy]],{{nb...|York Street, Covent Garden.}}|year=1870|page=287|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=KCBKAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA287|oclc=250333102|passage=There was a teacher who taught children to read under a '''calabash''' tree, and this teacher's name was Goso.}}
# The [[bottle gourd]] ([[calabash vine]], ''[[Lagenaria siceraria]]''), believed to have [[originate]]d in [[Africa]], which is grown for its fruit that are used as a [[vegetable]] and to make containers ''(sense 3)''; the fruit of this plant.
#: {{syn|en|dudhi|q1=India|lauki|q2=India}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=“L.”|title=The Calabash (''Cucurbita Lagenaria'')|magazine=The Visitor, or Monthly Instructor|location=London|publisher=[[w:Religious Tract Society|The Religious Tract Society]]; [...] sold by John Davis,{{nb...|at the Depository, 56, Paternoster Row, and 65, St. Paul’s Churchyard,; and by the booksellers.}}|month=January|year=1839|page=75|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=AAcbAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA75|oclc=740304389|passage=The '''calabash''', is a kind of gourd, and belongs to that family of twining plants which were called among the Hebrews, "wild vines," but by the botanists of modern times, the ''cucurbitaceæ'', or cucurbitaceous plants. [...] That playful variety of form and magnitude, so remarkable in the '''calabash''', renders it fit for all kinds of uses. Sometimes we have a globular base, terminating in a long neck, and then it answers the purpose of a bottle, and the American fastens it to his girdle, or the pommel of his saddle, when about to pass through regions which abound not in water.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=James A. Duke|authorlink=James A. Duke|author2=with Peggy-Ann K. Duke and Judith L. duCellie|chapter=Catalog of ‘Faith-based’ Farmaceuticals|title=Duke’s Handbook of Medicinal Plants of the Bible|location=Boca Raton, Fla.|publisher={{w|CRC Press}}|year=2008|page=233|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=UcLYLpwdcm8C&pg=PA233|isbn=978-0-8493-8202-4|passage='''CALABASH''' GOURD (''LAGENARIA SICERARIA'') [...] Appropriately bowing to AHP, the {{w|American Herbal Products Association}}, for the betterment of the herbal industry, I will now use its standardized common name, '''Calabash''' Gourd, a vine, not to be confused with the Calabash tree (Crescentia).}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Charlie Nardozzi and editors of the National Gardening Association|chapter=Sweet Corn and an A to T of Other Worthy Veggies|title=Vegetable Gardening for Dummies|series={{w|For Dummies}} series|edition=2nd|location=Hoboken, N.J.|publisher=[[w:Wiley (publisher)|Wiley Publishing]]|year=2009|section=part II (Vegging Out)|page=167|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=1ANqvBDcKNIC&pg=PA167|isbn=978-0-470-49870-5|passage=Asian gourds grow like cucumbers [...] and feature strong flavors that are often used in Indian, Chinese, and Southeast Asian soups and stews. Some types to try in your garden include the bitter gourd and '''calabash''' gourd.}}
# A container made from the [[mature#Adjective|mature]], [[dried#Adjective|dried]] [[shell#Noun|shell]] of the fruit of one of the above plants; also, a similarly [[shaped#Adjective|shaped]] container made from some other [[material#Noun|material]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Mungo Park|authorlink=Mungo Park (explorer)|chapter=Of Gold-dust, and the Manner in which It is Collected.—Process of Washing it.—{{nb...|Its Value in Africa.—Of Ivory.—Surprise of the Negroes at the Eagerness of the Europeans for This Commodity.—Scattered Teeth Frequently Picked up in the Woods.—Mode of Hunting the Elephant.—Some Reflections on the Unimproved State of the Country, &c.}}|title=Travels in the Interior Districts of Africa: Performed under the Direction and Patronage of the African Association, in the Years 1795, 1796, and 1797.{{nb...|[...] With an Appendix, Containing Geographical Illustrations of Africa: By Major Rennell.}}|location=London|publisher=Printed by [[w:William Bulmer (printer)|W[illiam] Bulmer and Co.]] for the author; and sold by [[w:George Nicol (bookseller)|G[eorge] and W[illiam] Nicol]],{{nb...|booksellers to His Majesty, Pall-Mall.}}|year=1799|page=302|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/b28766982/page/302/mode/1up|oclc=883610022|passage=[T]he manner of separating the gold from the sand, is very simple, and is frequently performed by the women in the middle of the town; for when the searchers return from the valleys in the evening, they commonly bring with them each a '''calabash''' or two of sand, to be washed by such of the females as remain at home.}}
#* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|chapter=Wheelbarrow” and “The Prophet|pages=65 and 105|pageref=65|passage=&#91;[https://archive.org/stream/mobydickorwhale01melv#page/65/mode/1up page 65]&#93; The people of his island of Rokovoko, it seems, at their wedding feasts express the fragrant water of young cocoanuts into a large stained '''calabash''' like a punchbowl; and this punchbowl always forms the great central ornament on the braided mat where the feast is held. [...] &#91;[https://archive.org/stream/mobydickorwhale01melv#page/105/mode/1up page 105]&#93; [I]t seemed to me that he was dogging us, but with what intent I could not for the life of me imagine. This circumstance, coupled with his ambiguous, half-hinting, half-revealing, shrouded sort of talk, now begat in me all kinds of vague wonderments and half-apprehensions, and all connected with the Pequod; and Captain Ahab; and the leg he had lost; and the Cape Horn fit; and the silver '''calabash'''; [...]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Olajire Olanlokun|chapter=3|editor=Karen Morrison|title=The Missing Calabash|location=Oxford; Gaborone, Botswana|publisher=[[w:Heinemann (publisher)|Heinemann Educational Publishers]]|year=1997|page=13|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Tv33QxD3E1UC&pg=PA13|isbn=978-0-435-89247-0|passage=Saje put the '''calabash''' in the king's hands. There was a hush from the crowd. The '''calabash''' was large and painted with beautiful designs. Few people had seen it up close.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Gabriel García Márquez|authorlink=Gabriel García Márquez|chapter=2|translator={{w|Edith Grossman}}|title=[[w:Living to Tell the Tale|Living to Tell the Tale: Translated from the Spanish]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Alfred A. Knopf}}|year=2003|isbn=978-1-4000-4134-3|passage=[...] we would pour water from the tank over ourselves with a '''calabash''' and finish by splashing on the Agua Florida from Lanman & Kemps [...]}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Mike Pflanz|title=Nigerian motorcyclists arrested for wearing pumpkin helmets|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/worldnews/africaandindianocean/nigeria/4161368/Nigerian-motorcyclists-arrested-for-wearing-pumpkin-helmets.html|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20100522002023/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/worldnews/africaandindianocean/nigeria/4161368/Nigerian-motorcyclists-arrested-for-wearing-pumpkin-helmets.html|archivedate=22 May 2010|newspaper={{w|The Daily Telegraph}}|location=London|publisher={{w|Telegraph Media Group}}|date=7 January 2009|issn=0307-1235|oclc=635239717|passage=[T]he motorcycle drivers say that new helmets are too expensive and instead have been found wearing pumpkin-like '''calabashes'''&nbsp;– dried fruit shells usually used to carry water&nbsp;– cooking pots tied with string or strips of rubber from old tyres.}}
# A calabash and its [[contents]]; as much as [[fill#Verb|fills]] such a container.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=T[homas] Coraghessan Boyle|authorlink=T. C. Boyle|chapter=The Niger|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=JbOaBAAAQBAJ&pg=PT481|title=[[w:Water Music (novel)|Water Music]]|location=Boston, Mass.|publisher=[[w:Little, Brown and Company|Little, Brown]]|year=1981|isbn=978-0-316-10467-8|location2=London|publisher2=[[w:Granta#Granta Books|Granta Books]]|year2=1998|isbn2=978-1-86207-155-1|passage=Amuta and the twelve-year-old have produced '''calabashes''' of palm wine and are busy pouring out healthy drams into the cupped hands of black and white alike.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Michael [D.] Jackson|chapter=Petra’s Letter|title=Harmattan: A Philosophical Fiction|location=New York, N.Y.; Chichester, West Sussex|publisher={{w|Columbia University Press}}|year=2015|section=part 2 (Harmattan)|page=122|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=5nsyBgAAQBAJ&pg=PA122|isbn=978-0-231-17234-9|passage=It was dusk by the time Yandi reappeared, carrying a '''calabash''' of parboiled rice and a peanut and chili sauce on his head.}}
# {{lb|en|musical instrument}} A [[musical instrument]], most commonly a [[drum#Noun|drum]] or [[rattle#Noun|rattle]], made from a calabash fruit.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Jerom Merolla da Sorrento|chapter=A Voyage to Congo, and Several Other Countries, Chiefly in Southern Africk.{{nb...|By Father Jerom Merolla da Sorrento, a Capuchin and Apostolic Missioner in the Year 1682. Made English from the Italian.}}|mainauthor={{w|John Pinkerton}}|title=A General Collection of the Best and Most Interesting Voyages and Travels in All Parts of the World;{{nb...|many of which are Now First Translated into English. Digested on a New Plan.}}|location=London|publisher=Printed for [[w:Longman|Longman, Hurst, Rees, Orme,and Brown]],{{nb...|Paternoster Row}}; and [[w:Cadell & Davies|Cadell and Davies]],{{nb...|in the Strand.}}|year=1814|volume=XVI|page=245|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=NmIOAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA245|oclc=156146323|passage=The inſtrument moſt in requeſt uſed by the Abundi, being the people of the kingdom of Angola, Matamba, and others, is the Marimba; it conſiſts of ſixteen '''calabaſhes''' orderly placed along the middle between two ſide-boards joined together, or a long frame, hanging about a man's neck with a thong. Over the mouths of the '''calabaſhes''' there are thin ſounding ſlips of red wood called Tanilla, a little above a ſpan long, which being beaten with two little ſticks, returns a ſound from the '''calabaſhes''' of ſeveral ſizes not unlike an organ.}}

